      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A hunting party in 1859 named Paradise Creek for its abundant water, timber, and bison, painting the area as an idyllic Kansas landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/paradise_kansas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1891 the Union Pacific Railroad listed its stop as Paradise (Ivamar), reflecting the growing preference for the creek‑named moniker.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/paradise_kansas",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Paradise sits on the north bank of Paradise Creek, a tributary of the Saline River, in Kansas’s Smoky Hills region.",
